jt-pve-storage-qnap (0.6.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed: a rollback on QTS was cut off after 60 seconds. It ran under the
    cluster storage lock, which Proxmox VE aborts after 60 seconds, while the
    NAS carried on writing the disk back. The lock is now held only to record
    that a rollback is running, and the rollback is waited for as before.
  * Fixed: a second rollback or clone started while one is running on the
    same NAS is refused with a message that says what is running, on which
    node and since when.
  * Changed: on QTS the plugin no longer makes linked clones or clones from a
    snapshot. A clone there copies the whole disk, and Proxmox VE runs a
    storage-side clone under the same 60 second limit. Clone a template with
    a full clone. Snapshots and rollback on QTS are unchanged, and so is
    QuTS hero.
  * Changed: a template made on QTS no longer keeps a snapshot of its own.
  * 27 unit tests added, 247 in total.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 12:57:25 +0800

jt-pve-storage-qnap (0.6.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed: QuTS hero h6.0 and later are refused when the storage is added.
    Measured on h6.0.1, the NAS refuses to create a target or a LUN for this
    plugin while everything the plugin reads still answers. 0.6.0 reported
    that as an unspecified error. It is now refused before anything is sent
    that the NAS would refuse, with a message naming the firmware.
    pve-qnap-api-probe says the same.
  * Fixed: a NAS upgraded to h6 under an existing storage says so. Creating
    or deleting a disk is refused by the NAS, and those refusals now name the
    firmware as well as the code.
  * Changed: the documents no longer say "any QuTS hero". h6.0 and later are
    listed as not supported, with a warning against upgrading a NAS in use.
  * Changed: a refused call now ends its message with a full stop.
  * 13 unit tests added, 220 in total.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 08:59:08 +0800

jt-pve-storage-qnap (0.6.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed: adding a storage with CHAP was refused. pvesm add with
    qnap-chap-username and qnap-chap-password answered "there is no CHAP
    secret", because the target was created from the stored secret and the
    secret is only stored after every check has passed. The target is now
    created from the secret that was just supplied. Adding a storage without
    CHAP was not affected.
  * Fixed: a GET request was sent to a NAS that reads POST bodies. The fallback
    for a firmware that ignores a POST body was taken for any answer without a
    result element, and two ordinary answers have none. It is now taken only
    when a session that was just issued is still not recognised, and never for
    a call that carries a secret.
  * A snapshot that a clone depends on cannot be deleted on QuTS hero. The
    refusal now quotes what the NAS answered and names the likely cause.
  * Messages no longer use a dash: a colon separates an operation from the
    reason it failed. The multipath drop-in carries the same change in its own
    comment, so a node with a qnapsan storage rewrites it once.
  * Added docs/LIMITS.md: the LUN, target and snapshot maxima QNAP publishes,
    with the source of each figure.
  * The plugin was driven through its whole lifecycle against a simulated NAS,
    which is what found the two defects above. NOT YET EXERCISED AGAINST
    HARDWARE: docs/TESTING.md lists every open item, and every 0.x release is a
    prerelease.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Thu, 01 Oct 2026 19:10:13 +0800

jt-pve-storage-qnap (0.1.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First release. Registers the storage type "qnapsan": one VM disk is one thin
    LUN on a QNAP NAS over iSCSI, with the NAS's own snapshots and clones.
  * NOT YET EXERCISED AGAINST HARDWARE: docs/TESTING.md lists every open item,
    and every 0.x release is a prerelease.
  * Requires Storage Manager V2, detected from authLogin.cgi's storage_v2 flag
    and refused rather than discovered later. QuTS hero is detected from is_zfs
    and uses instant clones, which is what makes a linked clone cheap; on QTS a
    clone copies.
  * Sizes are rounded UP to a whole GiB, because add_lun takes a capacity in GiB
    and has no finer step, and volume_size_info reports what the NAS actually
    has rather than what PVE asked for.
  * clone_qsnapshot and recover_snapshot fork on the NAS and report through a
    channel keyed by CGI name rather than by job, so both are serialised across
    the cluster with PVE's own storage lock.
  * Credentials are kept in /etc/pve/priv/storage/<id>.qnap rather than in
    storage.cfg, and every call is a POST, so no credential travels in a URL.
  * Snapshots carry no record of who took them on QTS, so ownership lives in the
    name. An operator's own scheduled snapshots are invisible to PVE and
    therefore cannot be deleted by a VM operation.

 -- Jason Cheng (Jason Tools) <jason@jason.tools>  Wed, 13 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0800
